1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,569
Every file on a server has an owner.

2
00:00:03,119 --> 00:00:06,149
Who you are decides what you can read, change, and run.

3
00:00:06,699 --> 00:00:10,411
Today you learn users, permissions, sudo, and S S H keys.

4
00:00:12,461 --> 00:00:15,874
First question on any server: who am I? Type: whoami.

5
00:00:16,424 --> 00:00:17,402
Now the detail.

6
00:00:17,402 --> 00:00:17,989
Type: id.

7
00:00:17,989 --> 00:00:19,097
Your user number,

8
00:00:19,097 --> 00:00:20,271
your group number,

9
00:00:20,271 --> 00:00:22,227
and every group you belong to.

10
00:00:22,777 --> 00:00:30,692
Every user is one line in slash etc slash password. Type: grep student /etc/passwd.

11
00:00:31,242 --> 00:00:33,281
Fields split by colons: the name,

12
00:00:33,281 --> 00:00:34,146
a placeholder,

13
00:00:34,146 --> 00:00:35,135
the user number,

14
00:00:35,135 --> 00:00:36,186
the group number,

15
00:00:36,186 --> 00:00:36,804
a comment,

16
00:00:36,804 --> 00:00:37,792
the home folder,

17
00:00:37,792 --> 00:00:39,028
and the login shell.

18
00:00:41,078 --> 00:00:44,747
Permissions live on every file. Type: ls -l.

19
00:00:45,297 --> 00:00:46,583
Read the first column.

20
00:00:46,583 --> 00:00:48,160
One character for the type,

21
00:00:48,160 --> 00:00:50,146
then three groups of three: owner,

22
00:00:50,146 --> 00:00:50,496
group,

23
00:00:50,496 --> 00:00:51,548
and everyone else.

24
00:00:52,098 --> 00:00:54,088
Each group is r for read,

25
00:00:54,088 --> 00:00:55,043
w for write,

26
00:00:55,043 --> 00:00:56,158
x for execute.

27
00:00:56,158 --> 00:00:57,432
A dash means no.

28
00:00:57,432 --> 00:01:01,969
So rw dash r dash dash r dash dash means the owner edits,

29
00:01:01,969 --> 00:01:03,959
everyone else only reads.

30
00:01:04,509 --> 00:01:09,224
Lock a secret down. Type: chmod 600 secret.txt.

31
00:01:09,774 --> 00:01:13,251
Check it. Type: ls -l secret.txt.

32
00:01:13,801 --> 00:01:15,871
Now the owner can read and write.

33
00:01:15,871 --> 00:01:17,501
Nobody else can even look.

34
00:01:17,501 --> 00:01:18,818
Six is four for read,

35
00:01:18,818 --> 00:01:20,009
plus two for write.

36
00:01:21,059 --> 00:01:25,177
Try running the script. Type: ./run.sh.

37
00:01:25,727 --> 00:01:32,660
Permission denied. The file is not executable yet. Fix it with: chmod +x run.sh.

38
00:01:33,210 --> 00:01:34,234
And run it again.

39
00:01:36,584 --> 00:01:41,320
Some files are not yours. Type: cat /etc/shadow.

40
00:01:41,870 --> 00:01:46,585
Permission denied. That file holds password hashes, and only root may read it.

41
00:01:47,635 --> 00:01:53,779
Root is the all powerful administrator. You borrow its power with sudo. Type: sudo whoami.

42
00:01:54,329 --> 00:01:55,777
The answer is root,

43
00:01:55,777 --> 00:01:57,759
for that one command only.

44
00:01:57,759 --> 00:01:58,978
Make a new user.

45
00:01:58,978 --> 00:02:01,113
Type: sudo useradd -m maria.

46
00:02:01,663 --> 00:02:04,777
Create a group. Type: sudo groupadd devs.

47
00:02:05,327 --> 00:02:12,239
Add Maria to it. Type: sudo usermod -aG devs maria. A G means append to a group.

48
00:02:12,789 --> 00:02:14,560
Check. Type: id maria.

49
00:02:15,110 --> 00:02:22,321
Now give her a file. Type: sudo chown maria:devs notes.txt. Owner colon group.

50
00:02:22,871 --> 00:02:27,116
Confirm with: ls -l notes.txt. New owner, new group.

51
00:02:29,166 --> 00:02:31,962
Passwords can be guessed.

52
00:02:31,962 --> 00:02:34,086
Keys are far safer.

53
00:02:34,086 --> 00:02:35,428
Make a pair.

54
00:02:35,428 --> 00:02:40,236
Type: ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -f demo_key -N,

55
00:02:40,236 --> 00:02:42,585
then two quote marks.

56
00:02:43,135 --> 00:02:49,577
Two files appeared. Type: ls -l demo_key demo_key.pub.

57
00:02:50,127 --> 00:02:52,505
The private key is rw only for you.

58
00:02:52,505 --> 00:02:54,543
It never leaves your computer.

59
00:02:54,543 --> 00:02:56,920
The dot pub file is the public key,

60
00:02:56,920 --> 00:02:57,871
safe to share.

61
00:02:58,721 --> 00:03:05,868
Read the public key. Type: cat demo_key.pub. One line. This is a throwaway demo key.

62
00:03:06,418 --> 00:03:13,134
A server trusts you when your public key is in its authorized keys file.

63
00:03:13,134 --> 00:03:15,186
Type: mkdir -p ~/.ssh.

64
00:03:15,736 --> 00:03:24,653
Add the key. Type: cat demo_key.pub, two arrows, then the authorized keys file.

65
00:03:25,203 --> 00:03:27,129
S S H is strict.

66
00:03:27,129 --> 00:03:30,861
Lock the folder with chmod 700,

67
00:03:30,861 --> 00:03:34,232
and the file with chmod 600.

68
00:03:34,232 --> 00:03:37,001
Type: chmod 700 ~/.ssh.

69
00:03:37,551 --> 00:03:45,081
Now the file. Type: chmod 600 ~/.ssh/authorized_keys.

70
00:03:45,631 --> 00:03:49,813
Check it. Type: ls -l ~/.ssh.

71
00:03:50,363 --> 00:03:56,272
Too open, and S S H refuses the key. That is the number one cause of key login failures.

72
00:03:58,322 --> 00:03:59,645
Four myths to clear up.

73
00:04:00,195 --> 00:04:03,163
Myth one: chmod 777 fixes everything.

74
00:04:03,163 --> 00:04:05,570
It gives everyone full access.

75
00:04:05,570 --> 00:04:07,255
It hides the problem,

76
00:04:07,255 --> 00:04:09,261
and creates a bigger one.

77
00:04:09,811 --> 00:04:15,379
Myth two: use sudo for every command. No. Use it only when you must. Least power wins.

78
00:04:15,929 --> 00:04:21,071
Myth three: share your private key to let a friend in. Never. Share the public key only.

79
00:04:21,621 --> 00:04:24,094
Myth four: root and sudo are the same.

80
00:04:24,094 --> 00:04:25,396
Root is the account.

81
00:04:25,396 --> 00:04:27,935
Sudo borrows its power for one command.

82
00:04:28,485 --> 00:04:30,619
Least power. Public key only.

83
00:04:32,669 --> 00:04:34,994
Here is how you check your own understanding.

84
00:04:35,544 --> 00:04:37,699
Say who you are with whoami and id.

85
00:04:38,249 --> 00:04:40,702
Read an ls dash l permission string.

86
00:04:41,252 --> 00:04:44,964
Change permissions with chmod, in numbers and with plus x.

87
00:04:45,514 --> 00:04:47,989
Create a user and group, and change an owner.

88
00:04:48,539 --> 00:04:51,995
Make an S S H key pair, and say which half is private.

89
00:04:52,545 --> 00:04:55,724
Set the right permissions on the S S H folder and file.

90
00:04:56,274 --> 00:04:57,895
To pass, you need all six.

91
00:04:59,945 --> 00:05:04,638
Your turn, in your virtual machine. Use a practice folder, and a practice user.

92
00:05:05,188 --> 00:05:08,474
One: run who am I and I D, and write down your groups.

93
00:05:09,024 --> 00:05:12,906
Two: make a file, and use ls dash l to read its permissions.

94
00:05:13,456 --> 00:05:17,083
Three: use chmod to make it private, then check it again.

95
00:05:17,633 --> 00:05:23,393
Four: write a tiny script, run it, see permission denied, then fix it with chmod plus x.

96
00:05:23,943 --> 00:05:24,921
Five: with sudo,

97
00:05:24,921 --> 00:05:26,510
create a user and a group,

98
00:05:26,510 --> 00:05:28,099
add the user to the group,

99
00:05:28,099 --> 00:05:29,383
and give them a file.

100
00:05:29,933 --> 00:05:31,967
Six: make a throwaway key pair,

101
00:05:31,967 --> 00:05:34,724
and set the S S H folder to seven hundred,

102
00:05:34,724 --> 00:05:36,824
and the key file to six hundred.

103
00:05:37,374 --> 00:05:40,680
Mark yourself on the rubric. Pause the video, and do it now.

104
00:05:56,230 --> 00:05:57,105
Quick check.

105
00:05:57,655 --> 00:06:00,279
What does chmod 600 give the owner?

106
00:06:00,829 --> 00:06:03,367
Which command borrows root power for one command?

107
00:06:03,917 --> 00:06:05,688
Which key file is safe to share?

108
00:06:06,238 --> 00:06:08,606
What should the S S H folder permission be?

109
00:06:09,156 --> 00:06:15,599
Objective covered: MC04-O04.

110
00:06:16,149 --> 00:06:18,218
Pause the video, and answer out loud.

111
00:06:28,768 --> 00:06:31,051
Next lesson: processes and signals.

112
00:06:31,601 --> 00:06:34,929
If this helped, like, share and subscribe, so you do not miss it.
